#!args scenario=Scenario_AllCards seed=42
#
# Reaction windows must not leak what a face-down Response card is, and the scoped skip must only
# silence the windows that exist to do that hiding.
#
# The host used to open a reaction window only for a faction that could actually play something. So
# "Waiting on Soviet input…" during a specific block window was proof that the Soviet held a
# face-down Response card matching exactly that event, and its absence was proof it held none — the
# prompt read out the hidden card. CardPlayRound.ShouldOpenReactionWindow now asks every faction
# holding an unrevealed Response card, whether or not it can react, so being asked stops proving
# anything and the player passes with Skip.
#
# That produces a lot of empty prompts, so a reaction prompt also offers "skip rest of turn step"
# and "skip rest of round" (ReactionSkipScope, answered here as `pass turnstep` / `pass round`).
# Those must NOT cost the player a reaction everyone can already see they hold: a face-up Status card
# is public knowledge, so its window leaks nothing and survives the skip. Only the windows that exist
# solely because of face-down cards go away.
#
# The three things pinned below, all in one removal-and-block setup shared with
# block_reaction_prevents_removal.qgc:
#
#   1. ALWAYS-ASK   the Soviet is asked with zero options, purely because it holds face-down cards.
#   2. SCOPED SKIP  after `pass round` the Soviet is not asked again this round.
#   3. CARVE-OUT    the US answers `pass round` too, but keeps being asked, because StatusRadar is a
#                   face-up Status card that genuinely matches the trigger.
#
# Germany and Japan never appear: Japan triggers the removals (a faction cannot block its own event)
# and Germany holds no unrevealed Response card under this scenario.
#
# Both removals target unit 110: a removed unit returns to the reserve pool and the next build takes
# its id back, so the second Navy is 110 again. Values are pinned to the seed in the #!args line;
# re-derive the id if unit allocation changes.
#
# Scope note: both removals land in the same turn, so this pins that a round-scope skip survives
# across separate reaction windows and that the carve-out holds — not that it survives a turn
# boundary. GameFlow.IsSkippingReactions compares against GameFlow.Round for that, and
# ChangeRoundChangeEvent drops the incoming faction's entry.
#
# Run:  tests/run-cli.sh tests/qgc/reaction_always_asked_and_scoped_skip.qgc

# The six mandatory opening discards — see opening_turn.qgc. Those carry the game through to Japan's
# turn (still round 1), which is where everything below happens.
auto 6

prompt                                  # synchronise with the running game

# ── Removal 1: prove the always-ask rule, and set two round-scope skips ──────────────────────────
#
# The US only starts with an Army in US East. Give it a Navy in the adjacent sea so both Destroyers
# ("a supplied US or UK Navy is about to be removed") and StatusRadar can match the removal.
force deployUnit --faction UNITED_STATES --country NORTH_ATLANTIC --deploy BUILD
api removeUnit --faction JAPAN --unit 110 --reason BATTLE

assert promptfaction == UNITED_KINGDOM
assert promptoptions == 1               # Destroyers really matches — an ordinary window
pass

assert promptfaction == SOVIET
assert promptoptions == 0               # (1) ALWAYS-ASK: nothing to play, asked anyway
pass round                              # the Soviet opts out for the rest of round 1

assert promptfaction == UNITED_STATES
assert promptoptions == 1               # StatusRadar — face up, so public knowledge
pass round                              # the US opts out too, and must still keep this window

assert promptfaction == ITALY
assert promptoptions == 0               # another always-ask window
pass

# The removal applies, opening its after-reaction windows. The Soviet is already gone from these.
assert promptfaction == UNITED_KINGDOM
pass
assert promptfaction == JAPAN           # (2) not SOVIET — its round-scope skip is in force
pass
assert promptfaction == ITALY
pass

# ── Removal 2: prove the skip holds for one faction and not the other ────────────────────────────
force deployUnit --faction UNITED_STATES --country NORTH_ATLANTIC --deploy BUILD
api removeUnit --faction JAPAN --unit 110 --reason BATTLE   # id reused from the removed Navy

assert promptfaction == UNITED_KINGDOM  # never skipped, so unaffected
assert promptoptions == 1
pass

# The load-bearing pair. Both the Soviet and the US answered `pass round`, and both hold face-down
# Response cards — but only the US also holds a face-up card that matches. So the Soviet's window is
# gone and the US's is not. FAILS as SOVIET if the skip is ignored, and as ITALY if the skip wrongly
# swallows a publicly visible reaction.
assert promptfaction == UNITED_STATES   # (3) CARVE-OUT: still asked, because Radar is face up
assert promptoptions == 1               # and it is still offered the card, not an empty window
pass

assert promptfaction == ITALY           # (2) the Soviet is still absent
assert promptoptions == 0
pass

quit
